  <info>
    <link type="guide" xref="index#design"/>
    <title type="sort">3</title>
    <link type="seealso" xref="canvas"/>
    <desc>Zoom, show rulers and a grid, and snap what you drag to the grid
    or to other elements.</desc>
  </info>

  <title>Zoom, rulers, grid and snapping</title>

  <section id="zoom">
    <title>Zoom</title>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Fit Label</gui>
        (<keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>0</key></keyseq>)</title>
        <p>Shows the whole label. A label opens this way.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Fit Width</gui>
        (<keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>9</key></keyseq>)</title>
        <p>Fills the width of the window; a tall label scrolls.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Actual Size</gui>
        (<keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>1</key></keyseq>)</title>
        <p>One printer dot to one screen pixel.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Zoom In</gui> and <gui>Zoom Out</gui></title>
        <p><keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>+</key></keyseq> (or
        <keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>=</key></keyseq>) and
        <keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>-</key></keyseq>, from 5% to 800%. With
        <key>Ctrl</key> held, the mouse wheel zooms about the
        pointer.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
    <p>These are in the <gui>View</gui> menu, and <gui>−</gui>,
    <gui>Fit</gui> and <gui>+</gui> are at the right of the menu bar. The
    zoom is shown at the right of the status bar, with <gui>(fit)</gui>
    while a fit is deciding it.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="rulers">
    <title>Rulers and the pointer's position</title>
    <p><guiseq><gui>View</gui><gui>Rulers</gui></guiseq>
    (<keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>R</key></keyseq>) shows rulers along the
    top and left of the canvas, measured from the label's top-left corner
    in the grid's unit. A red mark on each follows the pointer.</p>
    <p>Whether or not the rulers are shown, the status bar gives where the
    pointer is on the label, in the same unit.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="grid">
    <title>The grid</title>
    <p><guiseq><gui>View</gui><gui>Show Grid</gui></guiseq>
    (<keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>'</key></keyseq>) draws a grid over the
    label. It is never printed. <gui>Grid Settings…</gui> sets its spacing in
    inches, millimetres or dots, and how often a darker major line comes.
    Changing the unit converts the spacing, so 0.1 inch becomes 2.54 mm.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="snap">
    <title>Snapping</title>
    <terms>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Snap to Grid</gui>
        (<keyseq><key>Ctrl</key><key>Shift</key><key>'</key></keyseq>)</title>
        <p>Puts what you drag on the grid, whether the grid is shown or not.
        A moved selection's top-left corner goes to the nearest lines; a
        resized edge goes to the nearest line. New elements are placed on
        the grid too.</p>
      </item>
      <item>
        <title><gui>Snap to Elements</gui></title>
        <p>Lines what you drag up with the edges and centres of the other
        elements and of the label, and draws magenta guides while it
        does. Where an element is in reach it wins over the grid.</p>
      </item>
    </terms>
    <p>While dragging, hold <key>Alt</key> to stop snapping for as long as it
    is held, or <key>Shift</key> to keep a move along one axis. Only the
    pointer snaps: the arrow keys, typed positions, <gui>Align</gui> and
    <gui>Paste</gui> go exactly where they are told, so the arrows are the
    way to place something between grid lines.</p>
  </section>

  <p>The rulers, the grid, its settings and both snaps are remembered from
  one session to the next.</p>
</page>
